Run BSD? #define SVR4, LINUX

andrewmaxr@gmail.com wrote:
> I was wondering if anyone has had any experience installing slashem on
> os x?  Is there a doc available?  The source download includes
> instructions for many operating systems, but not mine.

Mac OS X is effectively a *BSD Unix-like, much like FreeBSD, NetBSD and
OpenBSD. Try following the instructions at
http://strategywiki.org/wiki/NetHack/Building - they derive from my
notes during a Mac OS X build of (vanilla) NetHack 3.4.3.

When building NetHack 3.4.3 or NetHack brass 040923, the best thing to
do is to pretend that your Mac OS X or *BSD system is not BSD, but is
somehow both Solaris and Linux. Thus, you should _not_ #define BSD,
however you leave the default settings of #define SYSV, #define SVR4.
If you #define BSD, the compiler will error at system.h line 86 because
the srandom declaration does not match the one in <stdlib.h>.

SLASH'EM inherits this mess from NetHack, but adds the complexity that
#define LINUX wants Linux kernel headers. My only attempt to build
SLASH'EM 0.0.7E7F2 over OpenBSD failed.

--Kernigh http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/User:Kernigh

----
As an example not to follow, check nethack 3.2.2 source: it assumes
loads of things based on the system type. Most of these assumptions are
obsolete and no longer reflect reality: POSIX functions are more useful
than older BSD versus SystemV differences, to the point that some
traditional bsd functions are now only supported through compatibility
libraries.
--http://www.openbsd.org/porting.html



